much of my identity in life now is  defined by loving science and I would  call myself a scientist I'm doing a PhD  and a lot of the opportunities that I've  had in life whether it's travelling or  anything else have been through science  and I feel like I've done quite well in  it I didn't always want to be a  scientist and I've sort of been  wondering how I ended up here  I definitely as a child didn't have any  particular desire to enter science or  any other academic field I think maybe  up to the ages of like 1415 I had just  no concept of even the idea of being a  scientist if you'd asked me back then  what I would do I probably would have  just said something like be a  hairdresser like that's the only sort of  career that I even had seen or thought  of I just because my parents weren't  scientists and I didn't really know any  scientists in my real life it was a very  alien idea for me at that stage so in  wandering this and wondering how I  became a scientist actually called my  mum and I asked her like why she thinks  I ended up loving science so much and  the thing that she said to me on the  phone was just that when I was a kid she  let me explore anything that I was  interested in so I do remember doing  lots of sort of after school activities  and clubs and none of them were science  related they would have been art or  music classes or like Bill guides and I  can take a things like that so I  definitely had a life that was full of  lots of different experiences and I had  this feeling that I could just if I was  interested in something I could pursue  it and I definitely had a lot of  interests my mom never directly bought  me like science books or something and  gave them to me and he said you read  this I like I want you to be a scientist  or she never really bought me those sort  of toys I see now for kids that are very  science learning back  but what she did do was make me feel  like for me anything was possible any  career that I wanted to do I 